Date: Wed, 17 Nov 93 2:23:40 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: E-prime

Kennita Watson writes about E'.  I lack E-xpertise
in the matter, but I'll give her queries a shot.

> 	1) "[The book] is written in E-prime."
> 	2) "What are you doing?"

E' concerns itself mostly with the form "is <noun>", as
in "A is A" (the ultimate egregious violation of E'),
and "is <adjective>" where it can be converted to an
action verb (eg "is red" --> "looks red").  Less often
it's useful to convert "is <verb>".  (1) can be
rewritten as "[We/they/the authors] wrote the book in
E'", admitting the possibility that they might have slipped
up at some point, so that the book wouldn't necessarily be
excepted by a rigorous E' parser.  I don't see any need to change 
(2).

In general, I don't think the "is <verb>" usually needs
conversion.  My opinion; I don't know what the "official"
E' rules are for "is <verb>".  Since Korzybski and
Peirce before him emphasized the "default heirarchy"
of rules/exceptions (now most well known as the class 
heirarchies in "object oriented programming"), it might violate
the spirit of the general philosophy to have rigid rules 
on the matter.  

The little spiel on the "Fair Witness" in Heinlein's
_Stranger in a Strange Land_ ("this side of the house
looks white") is a good explanation of how E' 
improves the accuracy of thinking.  I'd say E' improves
inductive thinking at some expense to parsimony and deduction 
(because the sentences are now in a form difficult to translate 
first-order logic).  The parsimony problem is mostly due to
the fact that "is" is so engrained in English that we lack
the morphemes to construct succinct sentences without it.
But we should start the conversion.  Since computers are vastly 
better at deductive logic than humans, there might come a future 
era where computers handle the rational arguments and humans the 
inductive "common sense".

> Not to
> mention that to ask "What is Being?" in a precise way requires already
> knowing what Being is in some sense, 

This seems to be a great example the of unproductive lines of
deduction E' allows us to avoid.  Korzybski and Peirce would
recommend accumulating the knowledge of ourselves first, and
building from that a language, in which the word "Being"
might or might not play a significant role.

_Feeling Good_ by Dr. David Burns, on Cognitive Therapy, is a good
example of applying GS/E'-style thought to one's thinking about
one's own self, with the specific application of overcoming 
depression.

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

