>    2) Please elaborate on your current activities.

A little stiff, perhaps, but notice how it focusses not on what the person
"is" doing, but on the meaning of their actions to themselves (which may
differ greatly from what anyone else might think those actions "were").

>   E-Prime sometimes sounds like Lt. Commander Data's script from Star
>Trek.

Not necessarily a bad thing.  It doesn't have to, though.

>> I would also appreciate a general rule for tranlating sentences in
>> progressive tenses, such as "I am going to the store.", "I was eating
>> lunch.", etc.

With E-Prime, as with Loglan (go on, ask me), you often need to completely
rethink what you want to express.  For example, "I was eating lunch,
when..." might be recast as "While eating lunch...".  Depending on the
situation, "I am going to the store" might be replaced by "I intend going
to the store", "I want to go to the store", "I need to buy some groceries",
"I need to go to work", "Bye", etc.


Indirectly related to E-prime, I came across this story a long time ago. 
(Does anyone know the original version and source?)

A missionary in Africa, learning the language of the natives, one day
knocked on a table and asked, "What do you call this?"  Getting six
different answers, he marvelled at the richness of a language which had six
different words for a table.  Only later did he find out that while one of
them had given the word for a table, another had given the word for wood,
another the word for a fist, another a hand, another the action of
knocking, and the sixth the sound of a knock.

