>Everybody knows that the harvest of corn will occur in November and 
>December and that there will be a glut of supple relative to demand.
>This has the effect of depressing the price of corn many more 
>going into December. Everybody knows this yet the price still sells off.

A bushel of corn in December isn't quite the same commodity as a
bushel of corn in, say, March, so one would expect them to have
different prices.  The differences are:

1. Somebody has to do the work to store the corn between December and
   March, and to maintain the equipment for storing it, buy the
   equipment, etc.

2. Money invested in the corn between December and March could have
   been invested elsewhere, so corn in March has to sell at a high enough
   price to provide about the same return.

Are the fluctuations larger than can be explained by the above?

If the market isn't efficient, then that's a money-making opportunity.
Anyone want to get into the corn-storing business?  Maybe we have
another point:

3. Storing corn for a living is BORING, and the workers demand
   proportionately higher wages. :-)

