Haven't read Miller, but DDFr summarizes the Icelandic experience in
_MoF_ for those who don't want to make a big project of it.

I don't have a chronology of the sagas here, although there might be
one in one of my books at home. I would recommend you read the Eddas
first if you haven't yet. If all you want is a closer look at how the
Althing and the wergild system worked, _The Saga of Burnt Njal_ has
enough on it. Bear in mind as you read that the frequent occurences of
"and then they all rode down to the Althing" are actually separated by
at least a year, but that the skald found that reciting the
proceedings of the various court cases was a quick and authoritative
narrative device. This makes the sagas seem like stories of an
incredibly bloodthirsty and contentious people on casual reading, but
a count of actual bloodlettings per year shows that, while they were
certainly proud and grim folk, and terrible when angered, they did not
go out of their way to slaughter each other. The murder rate and the
outlawry rate were actually quite low.

Reading projects like this are one quick answer to "why do you want to
live forever?". A few years ago I set myself to re-reading the
Greco-Roman classics: at the rate of about one Iliad-book per night at
bedtime, I went through the _Argosy_, the _Iliad_, the _Odyssey_, and
the _Aeneid_ (Fitzgerald's translations, exquisitely done) and petered
out with some Aeschylus and Sophocles, about 5 months' worth of
bedtimes. I then started reading them aloud to my children, so for a
while I was really up on the Heroic Era material ("Which Akhaian was
first ashore and first killed?" I'd ask and answer.)

