Date: Wed, 17 Nov 1993 08:32:12 -0600 (CST)
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: INTEREST? True Nyms

Jamie Dinkelacker:

> Aside from the flamebait puke of the COLORADO CRAZIE, the cypherpunks list
> doesn't seem to be an optimum forum for dicussion of false names, true
> nyms, multiple personality entropy, blah blah.
> 
> Yet, I find it a fascinating topic. Anyone here interested?

I confess that, although I subscribe to cypherpunks, I haven't been
following what passes for discussion on this issue there.  The
topics I like to see there relate to technical aspects of cryptology
(mainly cryptography though I recognize the importance of cryptanalysis),
the practical issues of crypto tools, and the prospects for the
development of Tim's "crypto-anarchy" -- the desirability of which
I accept.

This may indeed be a better forum for discussion of "softer" issues
because we can come at it from a particular perspective without
being accused of being maniacs (hopefully!).

I'll ignore the entire history of pseudonymous activity, because it
seems clear that electronic communication has made such activity
much more interesting than previously.  In this day when most
e-communication is via an ASCII stream, it's particularly easy
to maintain pseudonymous identities (though care must be taken
not to overly-indulge in similar communication style and usage,
and not to mix up what each persona should know [which apparently
L. Detweiler has failed to do {short, shameful confession: I do
love the pseudonym S. Boxx...}]).  As technology increases, it
may well be the case that synthesized video images for psuedonymous
communication could develop to the point of feasibility by the time
Panix is forwarding video e-mail to Extropians.

This issue is actually quite interesting -- the logistics of maintaining
pseudonyms that resist content analysis and correlation.  I expect
that a guide to the relevant techniques will appear eventually.

One other glurg:  it's important to monitor how secure a digital
nym actually is.  Right now, there would be definite limits I
would use when deciding what I could safely discuss pseudonymously,
because the current distribution mechanisms (anonymous remailers)
strike me as being rather insecure.  If the person running the
remailer became outraged enough, he or she apparently could
(anonymously!) reveal the linkage between a true name and 'nym.
Still, for most purposes, I gather that it is quite safe.  I find
the S. Boxx case interesting because it is a test of this -- by
outraging the very people with the expertise to blow a cover,
a valuable service is being done there.  But I digress.

The important question:  WHY would anybody want to use a 'nym?
By "'nym", I mean a _real_ pseudonym, not a pseudopseudonym, such
as Tim May uses at times (Klaus!) for rhetorical purposes.

I can think of three classes of reasons:
 * danger avoidance (as in Vinge's "True Names").  I will leave that
   reason for others to discuss.  Most people I talk to don't care
   about that -- and most "mundanes" would as soon see it banned
   even if means banning all secure cryptography.
 * reputation preservation
 * "psychological" reasons

Reputation preservation.  When we receive a message, we look at two
things:  Content, and Author.  The reason we look at the author and
take that into account when evaluating the content is that we have
a history with various Authors on the Net.  Often, we use the
Author to decide whether we should even read a message at all.
For example, the newsreader I use ('nn') has a facility where I
can search all of the zillions of messages in Usenet news for
articles by a particular person.  I use this facility regularly
because I am interested in what certain people have to say, regardless
of the forum or context.  Those people have positive reputations with
me.

However, the burden of Authorship (especially in a "BlackNet Investigations"
world) is such that a public persona is created -- and this can be
quite important, as we must continually make decisions about who we
will spend time associating with, and even who we will have economic
transactions with.  Our reputations preceed us, and it pays to keep
them in good shape.

Now, suppose that I "publish" a persona in which I value the power of
the free market, the Goodness of business, the Badness of regulations,
and so on.  Such a reputation could be a good idea to maintain if I
planned to do business with similarly-inclined people.  However, suppose
that I believed that the threats to the environment were such that they
override those basic beliefs, and say that I feel a need to speak about it.
I could urge environmental regulation and restrictive statist policies
under a pseudonym (say, "Green Lantern") and begin to build a distinct
reputation in such a way.  For the record, I don't have any such
beliefs or needs to express them -- and there is a penalty for using
'nyms: dilution of reputation.  In the extreme case, one could use
a new pseudonym for every message, which would make it impossible
to build any reputation capital at all.

Psychological reasons.  In the early days of BBSes (and presently,
for all I know), users would very frequently use "handles" instead
of their True Names when interacting in those fora.  Why?  That has
always puzzled me a bit.  I can only guess that there is a "fantasy"
aspect involved.  It could conceivably be rather liberating to
become somebody else, to present a persona that for whatever reason
doesn't manifest itself in one's "normal" life.  Thus a reclusive
bookish male librarian can get off on playing the electronic role
of a Dominatrix, or could vent repressed feelings of anger in flames
or diatribes without tarnishing a "primary" reputation.

derek
but do pseudonymous activities tend to demolish the self?  Does it
tend to fragment and suppress the ego?  Is it Buddhist therapy?

