|> 	2) "What are you doing?"
|> I would also appreciate a general rule for tranlating sentences in
|> progressive tenses, such as "I am going to the store.", "I was eating
|> lunch.", etc.

Everyone finds progressive constructions useful, and only the most
radical E-prime advocates insist on removing them. I don't know a good
general way; GS time-binding theory would suggest meta-speak like:

"I (11/17/1993 13:21) walk to the grocery store."

But that is really another issue, pointing out that "I (11/17/1993
13:21)" differs from "I (11/16/1993 13:21)" and from every "I
<timestamp>" with a different value of <timestamp>.

You can always translate passive voice to active, by naming the
actors, and usually make your writing more clear in the process:

"Ellis and Harper wrote the book in E-Prime."

Herein we see the virtue of E-prime: it makes you take responsibility
for your opinions, and assign responsibility where it belongs.
Contrast:

"Searle is clueless." with
"I find no evidence that Searle has a clue."

The second sentence allows and invites rational discourse better than
the flamish first sentence.

[...]But I digress.

You certainly did :-)
Well, we had a rousing debate-of-basics a while back over stuff like
